520: S2, and the circle bundle on these links is the Hopf bundle. In the
521: Gysin sequence, e generates H2(B), and integration H3(A)->H2(B) is
522: multiplication by1: evaluate on this linked S2 and its Hopf preimage.
523: Every other positive-degree group of B vanishes. Also pi1(B)=0 by the
524: homotopy sequence of the principal circle bundle.
525: 
526: Conjugating the negative-weight line converts the fixed normal bundle
527: to a positive-weight complex bundle O(1) plus O(-1). Its clutching loop
528: diag(z,z^-1) in SU2 bounds the explicit disc with SU2 coordinates
529: (a,b)=(r exp(i theta),sqrt(1-r²)); joining its centre to the identity
530: gives a nullhomotopy. Thus it is a trivial unitary rank2 bundle. Its
531: orbit normal bundle is a trivial oriented rank3 bundle. With this
532: trivialization the boundary is F times S2, and the Euler class is just
533: the meridian generator, with no F term.
534: 
535: Now Y=X/S1 is the union of B (truncated at that boundary) and F times
536: D3. The maps on H0 and H2 in Mayer--Vietoris, with domain H2 ordered
537: (F,meridian) and target(complement,tube), are respectively
538: 
539:     [1;-1],       [[0,1],[-1,0]].
540: 
541: The latter has determinant1. H4(boundary)=Z maps to zero and gives
542: H5(Y)=Z; every other positive intermediate group is zero. All matrices
543: and their Smith forms are printed in orbit_mv.out. Van Kampen gives
544: pi1(Y)=0. Thus Y is a homotopy5-sphere. Kervaire--Milnor1963, the table
545: on p505 and the Smale comparison stated immediately above it, gives
546: Theta5=0, so Y is diffeomorphic to S5. Haefliger1966 Corollary6.6 says
547: C_n^q=0 for n<2q-3; with n=2,q=3, and Theorem1.2 converting concordance
548: to isotopy, every embedding S2 in S5 is unknotted. This identifies the
549: orbit pair.
550: 
551: One may now construct the equivariant identification with the standard
552: circle action on the sphere boundary of D3 times D4. On the free
553: complement the principal circle bundle over S2 times D3 has Euler
554: generator and is the product Hopf bundle S3 times D3. On the fixed
555: tube it is the trivial positive-weight rank2 bundle just constructed.
556: Their two boundary identifications differ by a gauge map from S2 times
557: S2 to S1. Such a map has a global logarithm because H1(S2 times S2;Z)=0,
558: so it extends over a collar and removes the discrepancy. This gives an
559: equivariant diffeomorphism. It is also the specialization of Levine's
560: classification as stated in Harvey--Kerin--Shankar2020 Theorem4.1,
561: whose hypotheses are checked here: smooth codimension4 fixed set and
562: H2(F;Z) without2-torsion. Replacing the fixed tube S2 times D4 by the
563: free piece D3 times S3 doubles the free complement, giving S3 timesS3;
564: the quotient doubles S2 timesD3, giving S2 timesS3. The normal framing
565: is unique up to homotopy because pi2(SO4)=0. This is the asserted
566: conifold surgery. End of proof.
567: 
568: ## P3F.8. Proved: the actual equivariant family has a typed Hopf-gluing image
569: 
570: Let Phi be the actual positive parameter return map of the sphere
571: family, with the complex orientation, represented by an equivariant
572: Ehresmann trivialization. There is a Torelli diffeomorphism f of
573: S2 timesS3, supported in S2 timesD3, for which the oriented sphere
574: clutched by Phi is exactly the Hopf-lift gluing lambda(f) of P3F.4.
575: This statement identifies the morphism; it does not assume values of
576: the two integer invariants of f.
577: 
578: Proof. The parameter family carries the delta circle action. Its fixed
579: curves form a P1 bundle around the parameter circle; the two normal
580: weight lines have degree-1. The fixed-curve bundle is smoothly trivial
581: because PGL2(C) is connected. Each weight line over P1 timesS1 is
582: isomorphic as a smooth complex line bundle to the pullback of O(-1),
583: since H2(P1 timesS1;Z)=Z. Thus its equivariant normal disc bundle can
584: be trivialized around the parameter circle. Choose an Ehresmann
585: connection agreeing with that trivialization near the fixed set;
586: averaging over the compact circle makes it equivariant. Its return map
587: is the identity near the fixed tube.
588: 
589: Under P3F.7 write the sphere as
590: 
591:     (S2_y timesD4_u) union (D3_y timesS3_u),
592: 
593: with the circle the Hopf action on the u coordinate. Conjugating the negative
594: normal line reverses its real two-plane orientation; choose the
595: orientation of the y factor so that this identification preserves the
596: complex orientation of X. The restriction
597: of Phi to the free part is an equivariant map tilde f of D3_y timesS3_u
598: equal to the identity near its boundary. Its quotient is a map of
599: D3_y timesS2_u relative boundary. Extend it by the identity over the
600: other hemisphere of S3_y. This defines f:S3_y timesS2_u -> S3_y timesS2_u.
601: It fixes the H2 generator, since that generator is represented in the
602: identity hemisphere. It preserves orientation, so Poincare duality
603: makes it fix H3 as well. Consequently f is in the Torelli group.
604: 
605: Its Hopf lift A on S3_y timesS3_u equals tilde f on the first
606: hemisphere and the identity on the second. Any other lift differs by
607: a gauge map S2 timesS3 -> S1. Its H1 is zero, so that map has a global
608: logarithm and yields an isotopy of lifts.
609: 
610: Here is the cut-and-reglue comparison. In the standard sphere
611: boundary(D4_u timesD4_y), put
612: 
613:     H=D4_u timesS3_y,  K=S3_u timesD4_y,
614:     N=S3_u timesD3_y in boundary H=boundary K.
615: 
616: Our gluing convention identifies x in boundary H with A(x) in
617: boundary K, after the standard product-boundary identification.
618: The ball B7=D4_u timesD3_y has boundary equal to N union
619: (D4_u timesS2_y). Its complement in the rounded product boundary is
620: the other standard7-ball: this follows by taking the two hemispheres
621: of the y boundary and rounding their product collars. Regluing H to
622: K by A changes the identification only on N. Equivalently cut out B7
623: and reglue its boundary by the map equal to tilde f on N and the
624: identity on D4_u timesS2_y. That boundary map is precisely Phi in the
625: displayed decomposition of S6. The identity on the common collar
626: makes the two quotient constructions coincide pointwise outside N,
627: and their collar identifications coincide on N. Hence
628: 
629:     D7 union_Phi D7  =  (D4_u timesS3_y) union_A (S3_u timesD4_y)
630: 
631: as oriented smooth manifolds, with outward boundary orientation on
632: the first piece and the complex sphere orientation used to choose
633: the initial identification. This is lambda(f). End of proof.
634: 
635: The actual sixfold descent of P2.1 is
636: 
637:     H(t)delta, H(t)=-(4l1 log t+3l2 log(t-1))/(2pi i),
638: 
639: with cusp coefficient -p after carrying the collar integer l0.
640: P3K.6--7 constructs its real trace with the complete line clutch.
641: P3K.9 evaluates its characteristic pair as(-6,-24), including the
642: normal-frame comparison, and descends it to(-1,-4) for one parameter
643: loop. The forward geometric mapping-torus orientation and Jin's
644: reverse convention are both included in P3K.7.
645: 
